WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.450
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.740
In this footage, we have a variety of objects
that differ in geometry, and the goal is to

00:00:09.740 --> 00:00:12.910
place them into this box using an AI.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.829
Sounds simple, right?

00:00:14.829 --> 00:00:16.840
This has been solved long, long ago.

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:22.720
However, there is a catch here, which is that
this box is outside of the range of the robot

00:00:22.720 --> 00:00:27.939
arm, therefore, it has to throw it in there
with just the right amount of force for it

00:00:27.939 --> 00:00:30.039
to end up in this box.

00:00:30.039 --> 00:00:33.740
It can perform 500 of these tosses per hour.

00:00:33.740 --> 00:00:38.410
Before anyone misunderstands what is going
on in the footage here, it almost seems like

00:00:38.410 --> 00:00:43.910
the robot on the left is helping by moving
to where the object would fall after the robot

00:00:43.910 --> 00:00:45.910
on the right throws it.

00:00:45.910 --> 00:00:47.610
This is not the case.

00:00:47.610 --> 00:00:52.600
Here you see a small part of my discussion
with Andy Zeng, the lead author of the paper

00:00:52.600 --> 00:00:54.190
where he addresses this.

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:59.540
The results look amazing, and note that this
problem is much harder than most people would

00:00:59.540 --> 00:01:01.070
think at first.

00:01:01.070 --> 00:01:07.130
In order to perform this, the AI has to understand
how to grasp an object with a given geometry,

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:13.430
in fact, we may grab the same object at a
different side, throw it the same way, and

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.560
there would be a great deal of a difference
in the trajectory of this object.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:20.189
Have a look at this example with the screwdriver.

00:01:20.189 --> 00:01:25.710
It also has to take into consideration the
air resistance of a given object as well.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.340
Man, this problem is hard.

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:33.600
As you see here, initially, it cannot even
practice throwing because its reliability

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:35.360
in grasping is quite poor.

00:01:35.360 --> 00:01:41.899
However, after 14 hours of training, it achieves
a remarkable accuracy, and to be able to train

00:01:41.899 --> 00:01:47.639
for so long, this training table is designed
in a way that when running out of objects,

00:01:47.639 --> 00:01:51.460
it can restart itself without human help.

00:01:51.460 --> 00:01:53.049
Nice!

00:01:53.049 --> 00:01:58.630
To achieve this, we need a lot of training
objects, but not any kind of training objects.

00:01:58.630 --> 00:02:01.499
These objects have to be diversified.

00:02:01.499 --> 00:02:07.079
As you see here, during training, the box
position enjoys a great variety, and the object

00:02:07.079 --> 00:02:09.820
geometry is also well diversified.

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:15.720
Normally, in these experiments, we are looking
to obtain some kind of intelligence.

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:20.970
Intelligence in this case would mean that
the AI truly learned the underlying dynamics

00:02:20.970 --> 00:02:25.590
of object throwing, and not just found some
good solutions via trial and error.

00:02:25.590 --> 00:02:31.310
A good way to test this would be to give it
an object it has never seen before and see

00:02:31.310 --> 00:02:34.530
how its knowledge generalizes to that.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:36.060
Same with locations.

00:02:36.060 --> 00:02:42.030
On the left, you see these boxes marked with
orange, this was the training set, but, later,

00:02:42.030 --> 00:02:50.150
it was asked to throw it into the blue boxes,
which is something it has never tried before…and…look!

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:52.340
This is excellent generalization.

00:02:52.340 --> 00:02:53.440
Bravo!

00:02:53.440 --> 00:02:57.150
You can also see the success probabilities
for grasping and throwing here.

00:02:57.150 --> 00:03:02.980
A key idea in this work is that this system
is endowed with a physics-based controller,

00:03:02.980 --> 00:03:07.200
which contains the standard equations of linear
projectile motion.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.590
This is simple knowledge from high-school
physics that ignores several key real-life

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:15.510
factors, such as the effect of aerodynamic
drag.

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:20.370
This way, the AI does not have to learn from
scratch and can use these calculations as

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.990
an initial guess, and it is tasked with learning
to account for the difference between this

00:03:24.990 --> 00:03:28.230
basic equation and real-life trajectories.

00:03:28.230 --> 00:03:33.240
In other words, it is given basic physics
and is asked to learn advanced physics by

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:34.590
building on that.

00:03:34.590 --> 00:03:35.950
Loving this idea.

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:41.220
A simulation environment was also developed
for this project where one can test the effect

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:46.850
of, for instance, changing the gripper width,
which would be costly and labor-intensive

00:03:46.850 --> 00:03:48.120
in the real world.

00:03:48.120 --> 00:03:51.820
Of course, these are all free in a software
simulation.

00:03:51.820 --> 00:03:53.270
What a time to be alive!

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:56.790
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

